    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a high-ceiling core, anchored by **Yordan Alvarez** (82.97) and **Trea Turner** (81.27), our clear must-start pillars. **Yordan Alvarez** benefits from a favorable matchup against Tyler Phillips, where his elite 0.473 xwOBA and platoon advantage drive him to our top score. Meanwhile, **Trea Turner** is perfectly positioned to capitalize on his elite sprint speed; he faces Justin Wrobleski, and with a slow-delivery pitcher on the mound and favorable wind conditions, the stolen base potential here is massive. **Kyle Schwarber** (76.62) rounds out our top-tier plays, also benefiting from the Wrobleski matchup and wind conditions that favor his power profile. We are also leaning heavily into **Casey Schmitt** (63.92) and **Lars Nootbaar** (64.23), both of whom see their scores buoyed by significant platoon advantages against Luinder Avila and Walbert Ure\u00f1a, respectively.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Carson Benge** (56.34), who narrowly missed the cut behind **Nelson Velazquez** (56.53). While **Carson Benge** offered a respectable floor, the optimizer prioritized the higher projected raw power and order placement of **Nelson Velazquez** against Ure\u00f1a. Elsewhere, we are monitoring significant volatility for **Dillon Dingler** (57.51) and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (58.43). Both players possess high-upside profiles\u2014particularly **Pete Crow-Armstrong** due to his base-stealing opportunities against Framber Valdez\u2014but they are tethered to a 41% rain risk and unfavorable wind-in conditions, which capped their potential and necessitates a close eye on the pre-game weather reports. We remain cautious with **Kazuma Okamoto** (44.09), whose score is suppressed by a difficult encounter with Drew Rasmussen, leaving him as a marginal start for today\u2019s rotation.",
